  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency assistance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2023, Australia&#x2019;s core funding supported UNHCR global operations. As global displacement increased, Australian funding contributed to the provision of emergency assistance to more than 38 million people across 165 countries. UNHCR&#x2019;s response included cash assistance, shelter, relief items, health care, protection assistance, nutrition, access to water, sanitation and hygiene and emergency education. Cash assistance was provided to 7.3 million people, 14.6 million people benefited from UNHCR-supported health services, 1.5 million women and girls received sexual and reproductive health services, 2.9 million people received shelter and housing assistance and 8.1 million people received non-food items, including core relief items.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Assistance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2024, the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR) sought to protect and assist a global population of 129.9 million forcibly displaced and stateless people. 
&#x200d;
As global displacement increased, Australian funding contributed to the provision of emergency assistance to 5.7 million people in 43 emergency situations. Activities supported refugees, asylum-seekers, other people in need of international protection, internally displaced persons (IDPs), returnees (refugees and IDPs), stateless persons, and others of concern to UNHCR.  
&#x200d;
Beyond emergency assistance, responses in 2024 included shelter, relief items, health care, protection and legal assistance, nutrition, access to water, sanitation and hygiene, education and cash assistance to support sustainable returns. 18.4 million people received protection services, 4.6 million people were supported to obtain civil, identity or legal status documentation, and 6 million people received non-food items.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
